1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,006
Nowadays, we use different products for personal cleanliness,

2
00:00:05,006 --> 00:00:09,460
laundry, dishwashing and household cleaning

3
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:13,140
but this is very much a 20th-century development.

4
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:17,509
The origins of cleanliness date back to prehistoric times.

5
00:00:17,509 --> 00:00:23,334
Since water is essential for life, the earliest people lived near water

6
00:00:23,334 --> 00:00:27,380
and knew something about its cleansing properties

7
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:31,100
at least that it rinsed mud off their hands.

8
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:35,000
During the excavation of ancient Babylon

9
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:41,810
evidence was found that soapmaking was known as early as 2800 BC.

10
00:00:41,810 --> 00:00:45,149
Archaeologists discovered cylinders made of clay,

11
00:00:45,149 --> 00:00:51,270
with inscriptions on them saying that fats were boiled with ashes.

12
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:53,580
This is a method of making soap

13
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:56,970
though there's no reference to the purpose of this material.

14
00:00:56,970 --> 00:01:03,420
The early Greeks bathed for aesthetic reasons and apparently didn't use soap.

15
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:07,840
Instead, they cleaned their bodies with blocks of sand

16
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:11,378
pumice and ashes, then anointed themselves with oil,

17
00:01:11,378 --> 00:01:18,400
and scraped off the oil and dirt with a metal instrument known as a strigil.

18
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:21,780
They also used oil mixed with ashes.

19
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:26,050
Clothes were washed without soap in streams.

20
00:01:26,050 --> 00:01:34,195
The ancient Germans and Gauls are also credited with discovering how to make a substance called 'soap',

21
00:01:34,195 --> 00:01:41,860
made of melted animal fat and ashes. They used this mixture to tint their hair red.

22
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:47,155
Soap got its name, according to an ancient Roman legend,

23
00:01:47,155 --> 00:01:53,510
from Mount Sapo, where animals were sacrificed, leaving deposits of animal fat.

24
00:01:53,510 --> 00:01:58,000
Rain washed these deposits, along with wood ashes

25
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:01,190
down into the clay soil along the River Tiber.

26
00:02:01,190 --> 00:02:07,530
Women found that this mixture greatly reduced the effort required to wash their clothes.

27
00:02:07,530 --> 00:02:11,565
As Roman civilisation advanced, so did bathing.

28
00:02:11,565 --> 00:02:16,300
The first of the famous Roman baths

29
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:21,940
supplied with water from their aqueducts, was built about 312 BC.

30
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:27,290
The baths were luxurious, and bathing became very popular.

31
00:02:27,290 --> 00:02:30,737
And by the second century AD,

32
00:02:30,737 --> 00:02:37,630
the Greek physician Galen recommended soap for both medicinal and cleansing purposes.

33
00:02:37,630 --> 00:02:46,613
After the fall of Rome in 467 AD and the resulting decline in bathing habits,

34
00:02:46,613 --> 00:02:53,200
much of Europe felt the impact of filth on public health.

35
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:55,469
This lack of personal cleanliness

36
00:02:55,469 --> 00:03:03,185
and related unsanitary living conditions were major factors in the outbreaks of disease in the Middle Ages,

37
00:03:03,185 --> 00:03:07,270
and especially the Black Death of the 14th century.

38
00:03:07,270 --> 00:03:11,863
Nevertheless, soapmaking became an established craft in Europe,

39
00:03:11,863 --> 00:03:17,030
and associations of soapmakers guarded their trade secrets closely.

40
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:21,940
Vegetable and animal oils were used with ashes of plants

41
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:25,360
along with perfume, apparently for the first time.

42
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:30,938
Gradually more varieties of soap became available for shaving and shampooing,

43
00:03:30,938 --> 00:03:33,980
as well as bathing and laundering.

44
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:42,030
A major step toward large-scale commercial soapmaking occurred in 1791, when a French chemist,

45
00:03:42,030 --> 00:03:50,080
Nicholas Leblanc, patented a process for turning salt into soda ash, or sodium carbonate.

46
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:58,130
Soda ash is the alkali obtained from ashes that combines with fat to form soap.

47
00:03:58,130 --> 00:04:04,280
The Leblanc process yielded quantities of good-quality, inexpensive soda ash.

48
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:08,087
Modern soapmaking was born some 20 years later,

49
00:04:08,087 --> 00:04:14,272
in the early 19th century, with the discovery by Michel Eugène Chevreul,

50
00:04:14,272 --> 00:04:17,220
another French chemist, of the chemical nature

51
00:04:17,220 --> 00:04:21,410
and relationship of fats, glycerine and fatty acids.

52
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:27,020
His studies established the basis for both fat and soap chemistry

53
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:29,270
and soapmaking became a science.

54
00:04:29,270 --> 00:04:34,200
Further developments during the 19th century made it easier

55
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:35,910
and cheaper to manufacture soap.

56
00:04:35,910 --> 00:04:41,640
Until the 19th century, soap was regarded as a luxury item

57
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:44,310
and was heavily taxed in several countries.

58
00:04:44,310 --> 00:04:47,026
As it became more readily available,

59
00:04:47,026 --> 00:04:49,400
it became an everyday necessity

60
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:54,270
a development that was reinforced when the high tax was removed.

61
00:04:54,270 --> 00:05:00,670
Soap was then something ordinary people could afford, and cleanliness standards improved.

62
00:05:00,670 --> 00:05:05,148
With this widespread use came the development of milder soaps for bathing

63
00:05:05,148 --> 00:05:12,610
and soaps for use in the washing machines that were available to consumers by the turn of the 20th century.
